{
  "$comment": "Shared canonicalisation fixture for the Spark pairing invitation. The runtime signs this form (runtime/crates/sparkd/src/pairing.rs, InvitationPayload::canonical_without_signature) and the Apple app must rebuild it exactly (SparkBootstrap/FirstOwnerPairing.swift, PairingInvitationPayload.canonicalBytes) or it cannot verify an invitation it was genuinely issued. This form is deliberately NOT the one the release manifest uses: see the algorithm note below.",
  "algorithm": [
    "drop nothing: the signature member is present with a null value",
    "keep the struct's declaration order; do NOT sort members by key",
    "omit an absent optional member entirely (cluster_id, local_endpoint, remote_endpoint)",
    "serialise compactly with comma and colon separators and no whitespace",
    "escape only the quote, the backslash, and control characters below U+0020",
    "never escape the solidus; emit non-ASCII as raw UTF-8"
  ],
  "$why_this_differs_from_the_manifest": "The manifest is signed over sorted keys with nulls dropped; this is signed over declaration order with signature:null present. Two canonical forms in one system is not a good state of affairs, and unifying them is a contract change that would invalidate every invitation already in flight. It is recorded as an open item in ADR-019. What matters is that both sides reproduce the form the runtime actually signs, rather than the one that would be tidier.",
  "cases": [
